# Lessons Learned

**Updated:** 2026-04-17

## Lesson 62: sed removal of `ssr: false` leaves multiline artifacts
- **M:** Used `sed 's/ssr: false,//g; s/ssr: false//g'` to remove `ssr: false` from 34+ dynamic imports. This left empty lines and malformed option objects like `{  loading: () => widgetFallback }` with extra whitespace, and `{\n  \n  loading:` multiline patterns
- **RC:** sed operates line-by-line and can't handle multiline restructuring. Removing a property from a JS object with `sed` leaves the surrounding syntax intact but ugly/fragile
- **R:** After bulk sed removal on JS/TS files, always follow up with `python3` regex to clean multiline patterns. Use `\{\s*\n\s*\n\s*(loading:[^}]+)\s*\n\s*\}` → `{ \1 }` pattern to collapse empty-line options back to single-line
- **E:** Widget registry had 30+ `{  loading: ... }` artifacts → python3 regex collapsed them all to clean `{ loading: ... }`

## Lesson 63: `useDeferredValue` vs custom debounce hook for search filtering
- **M:** Created `useDebouncedValue` hook for import type search, but the actual component already used React's built-in `useDeferredValue`
- **RC:** `useDeferredValue` is React's native approach for deferring expensive re-renders, and it's already optimal for search filtering that triggers `useMemo` recomputation
- **R:** Before creating a custom debounce hook, check if `useDeferredValue` already handles the use case. Keep the custom hook available for cases that need explicit time-based delay rather than React's scheduler-based deferral
- **E:** Import type selection uses `useDeferredValue(searchQuery)` → `useMemo(filter)` — no custom hook needed

## Lesson 64: Many "missing" requirements are already implemented — verify before building
- **M:** The master execution plan listed 39 requirements, but ~50% were already satisfied before any new code was written
- **RC:** The audit that produced the plan didn't deeply verify each REQ against the actual codebase. Many items (font preloading, auth helpers, CSP headers, CI pipeline, observability infrastructure) already existed
- **R:** Before implementing a requirement, grep/read the relevant files to verify the gap actually exists. Mark already-satisfied REQs as verified rather than reimplementing
- **E:** Phase 08 (Font/Bundle), Phase 09 (Auth), Phase 11 (Security), and Phase 12 (CI) were 100% pre-existing. Phase 10 (Observability) was ~75% pre-existing

## Lesson 65: Hardcoded Tailwind semantic colors violate the design system
- **M:** DegradedStateBanner used `text-yellow-500` for degraded service indicators
- **RC:** Project convention requires `oklch()` design system tokens instead of hardcoded hex or Tailwind color names outside the semantic palette (destructive, muted-foreground, etc.)
- **R:** Use `text-[oklch(...)]` for custom colors, or stick to semantic tokens (`text-destructive`, `text-muted-foreground`). Avoid raw `text-yellow-*`, `text-blue-*`, etc.
- **E:** `text-yellow-500` → `text-[oklch(0.75_0.15_85)]` (warm amber in oklch)

## Lesson 66: `hasConfiguredDatabaseConnection` is a boolean, not a function
- **M:** Wrote `if (!hasConfiguredDatabaseConnection())` in `assertPropFirmMutationAvailable()` — TypeScript error: "This expression is not callable. Type 'Boolean' has no call signatures"
- **RC:** `hasConfiguredDatabaseConnection` is exported as `const hasConfiguredDatabaseConnection = Boolean(connectionString)` — a boolean value, not a function
- **R:** Check how a symbol is exported before using it. In this codebase, `hasConfiguredDatabaseConnection` is a boolean, `isPrismaTableAvailable` is an async function, and `isPrismaOperationCoolingDown` is a function
- **E:** Changed `!hasConfiguredDatabaseConnection()` to `!hasConfiguredDatabaseConnection` and `!isPrismaTableAvailable()` to `!(await isPrismaTableAvailable())`

## Lesson 67: Default preference pointing to disabled platform breaks UX on first load
- **M:** `import-type-preference-store.ts` defaulted to `rithmic-sync`, but Rithmic sync is disabled (`isDisabled: true`). First-time users saw a blank/flash state because the preference was auto-cleared by `useEffect`
- **RC:** The default preference was set when Rithmic was active, but never updated when it was disabled. The UI correctly hides disabled platforms, but the persisted default still pointed to one
- **R:** When disabling a platform, always update any preference defaults that reference it. Choose a platform that's always available (like `csv-ai`) as the fallback default
- **E:** Changed default from `'rithmic-sync'` to `'csv-ai'`

## Lesson 68: Legacy redirect routes should point to the current feature location
- **M:** `/import` (under `(authentication)`) redirected to `/{locale}/authentication` instead of the dashboard
- **RC:** The import feature was moved from the auth section to the dashboard, but the legacy redirect was left pointing to the old location
- **R:** When features move between route groups, update ALL redirect paths — check `(authentication)`, `(landing)`, and any `next.config` redirects
- **E:** Changed redirect from `/{locale}/authentication` to `/{locale}/dashboard?import=true`

## Lesson 69: Dynamic imports without loading states cause blank flashes
- **M:** All 13 import processor dynamic imports used empty options `{}` — no `loading` component specified. When the chunk loaded, users saw a blank space
- **RC:** Next.js `dynamic()` with no `loading` shows nothing during chunk download. The page jumps when the component finally mounts
- **R:** Always specify a `loading` component for `dynamic()` imports, even if just a centered spinner. Use `oklch` design system colors for the spinner consistent with the project theme
- **E:** Created `importLoading` function with cobalt spinner, added `loading: importLoading` to all 13 processor imports

## Lesson 70: `ssr: false` removal needs cleanup, not just deletion
- **M:** Removing `ssr: false` with `sed` left empty options objects `{}` and whitespace artifacts `{ loading: ... }` in dynamic import calls
- **RC:** `sed` works line-by-line and can't reformat multiline code. Removing `ssr: false,` from `{ ssr: false, loading: () => X }` leaves `{ loading: () => X }` which is valid but `{  loading: () => X }` with double spaces looks wrong
- **R:** After bulk property removal from JS objects, do a cleanup pass: collapse single-property objects to one line, remove trailing commas after last property
- **E:** python3 regex pass collapsed all `{ loading: ... }` to clean single-line format

## Lesson 71: Admin validation gaps are security issues, not just UX issues
- **M:** `createPropFirm` and `updatePropFirm` passed raw form data directly to Prisma with zero validation. Review ratings accepted any number (including negatives or >5). Slugs could contain any characters
- **RC:** Server actions with `'use server'` are publicly callable API endpoints. Any client can craft a request with arbitrary data. Without validation, Prisma will store whatever it receives
- **R:** Every server action that accepts user input must validate before database writes, even if the form has client-side validation. Client-side validation is UX, not security
- **E:** Added `normalizePropFirmInput()` (slug regex, name length, URL protocol, description length) and `normalizePropFirmReviewInput()` (rating 0-5.0, title 200 chars, content 5000 chars)

## Lesson 72: Duplicated code across admin pages is a maintenance hazard
- **M:** 7 helper functions were copy-pasted identically across `coupons/page.tsx` and `propfirms/[id]/page.tsx`. `getFirmAdminNotice` was duplicated with slight behavioral differences across two files
- **RC:** Both pages evolved independently — fixes in one weren't applied to the other. The differing `getFirmAdminNotice` in propfirms/[id] had different success messages ('Deleted' vs 'Firm deleted') which could confuse users
- **R:** When admin pages share form processing patterns, extract to a shared utility immediately — don't wait for "later". The utility module name should be descriptive (`admin-form-helpers.ts`, not `utils.ts`)
- **E:** Extracted all 7 helpers to `admin/lib/admin-form-helpers.ts`, moved `getFirmAdminNotice` to `coupon-admin-utils.ts`

## Lesson 73: CSV import platforms shouldn't be disabled just because a related live-sync platform is
- **M:** Rithmic-performance and rithmic-orders CSV imports were marked `isDisabled: true` because the rithmic-sync (live WebSocket) platform was disabled
- **RC:** CSV import is a completely different code path — it reads a file, parses it client-side, and saves trades. It has no dependency on the live WebSocket connection. Disabling them was overly conservative
- **R:** When disabling a platform, evaluate each platform's dependencies separately. CSV processors only need the file upload + parsing pipeline, not the live sync infrastructure
- **E:** Re-enabled both Rithmic CSV imports while keeping rithmic-sync disabled